.TH "TRACE\-RECURSE\.SH" "" "March 2026" ""
.SH "NAME"
\fBtrace\-recurse\.sh\fR
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBtrace\-recurse\.sh\fR \fIinput\.dot\fR \fItoken\fR \fIoutput\fR \fIfilter\fR \fIhistory\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBtrace\-recurse\.sh\fR is the recursive traversal helper for \fBtrace\.sh\fR\. It scans a Graphviz DOT file for all edges and nodes involving a given \fItoken\fR, appends matching lines to an accumulator file, and calls itself recursively for each newly discovered downstream node\.
.P
For each line in \fIinput\.dot\fR that mentions \fItoken\fR, the script distinguishes three cases\. If the line is an edge (\fB\->\fR) and \fItoken\fR is the source, the target node is extracted and, provided it has not been visited before (as recorded in \fIhistory\fR) and both the source and target entity types appear in \fIfilter\fR, the edge is appended to \fIoutput\fR and the script recurses with the target as the new token\. If the line is a node declaration for \fItoken\fR and the token's entity type appears in \fIfilter\fR, the node line is appended to \fIoutput\fR\. Edge lines in which \fItoken\fR is the target rather than the source are silently ignored, confining the traversal to forward (downstream) edges only\.
.P
The script enforces a caller restriction: it inspects its parent process name and refuses to run unless it was invoked by \fBtrace\.sh\fR or by another instance of itself\. This prevents accidental direct invocation\.
.P
This script is not intended to be called directly\. Use \fBtrace\.sh\fR instead\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
.TP
\fIinput\.dot\fR
Path to the Graphviz DOT file being traversed\. Passed unchanged through each level of recursion\.
.TP
\fItoken\fR
The node identifier currently being traced, of the form \fBEntityType\.id\fR\. At the top level this is the original search token supplied to \fBtrace\.sh\fR; at deeper levels it is a downstream node discovered during traversal\.
.TP
\fIoutput\fR
Path to the accumulator file to which matching node and edge lines are appended\. Shared across all recursive calls\.
.TP
\fIfilter\fR
A space\-separated list of entity type names\. Only nodes and edges whose source and target types both appear in this list are written to \fIoutput\fR\.
.TP
\fIhistory\fR
A string recording the node identifiers that have already been visited during the current traversal, used to detect cycles and prevent infinite recursion\. Each visited node identifier is concatenated onto this string before the next recursive call\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
This command takes no option flags\. All arguments are positional and mandatory\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Returns 0 on success\.
.P
Returns \-1 if the script is not called from \fBtrace\.sh\fR or \fBtrace\-recurse\.sh\fR\.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
No environment variables are used\.
.SH "RETURN VALUE"
None\. Results are written to the \fIoutput\fR accumulator file\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
This script is not intended to be invoked directly\. It is called automatically by \fBtrace\.sh\fR\. A representative internal call takes the form:
.IP "" 4
.nf
trace\-recurse\.sh provenance\.dot Containers\.container_505627104 \e
    /tmp/tmp\.abc123 "Applications Containers" \e
    Containers\.container_505627104
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "FILES"
.TP
\fBtrace\.sh\fR
The parent script that performs argument parsing, option handling, and final output assembly\. \fBtrace\-recurse\.sh\fR must be called from \fBtrace\.sh\fR or from itself\.
